cue,start_seconds,end_seconds,chapter,source_labels,transition,voice_en
1,0,10,1,BS08_001;BS08_005;BS08_070;BS08_008,cut,"At this height, every uphill step asks more of the body. Yet inside a mountain cave, ancient people left evidence of repeated returns."
2,10,20,1,BS08_020;BS08_040;BS08_054;BS08_018,cut,"Their clues lay among thousands of animal bones. One small fragment would lead investigators back to the people themselves, and a remarkable survival story."
3,20,30,1,BS08_004;BS08_109;BS08_091;BS08_120,cut,"This is Baishiya. The question is how a human lineage made a demanding plateau part of its world, long before any written memory."
4,30,40,1,BS08_004;BS08_003,cut,"The cave opens above a valley on the northeastern Tibetan Plateau. Around it, limestone cliffs rise over slopes where grass and shrubs meet."
5,40,50,1,BS08_005;BS08_037,cut,"Its entrance stands roughly three thousand two hundred eighty metres above sea level. Height changes something invisible, even on a perfectly clear day."
6,50,60,1,@ALTITUDE;BS08_007,cut,The proportion of oxygen in the air stays nearly the same. Falling air pressure means each breath delivers less oxygen than at lower elevations.
7,60,70,1,BS08_103;BS08_031,cut,"Now add physical work, difficult ground, and changing conditions. Finding food matters twice: it supplies the body, and obtaining it costs precious energy."
8,70,80,1,BS08_008;BS08_003,cut,"A cave can offer shelter. It cannot bring a meal to its entrance, carry a carcass uphill, or decide which resource deserves the effort."
9,80,90,1,BS08_040;BS08_043,cut,"Those decisions leave no diary. They can leave other records: a sharp cut, a fresh fracture, a surface changed by repeated contact."
10,90,100,1,BS08_010;BS08_047,cut,"For investigators, a broken bone may preserve both an animal's remains and the trace of someone who knew how to use them."
11,100,110,1,BS08_011;BS08_015,cut,"But the plateau does not surrender its story at once. First, we need to separate what the cave contains from what we merely imagine."
12,110,120,1,BS08_012;BS08_001,cut,Then a surprising picture emerges. Survival here was about more than reaching the mountains. It was about finding ways to keep returning.
13,120,130,2,BS08_013;BS08_016,dissolve,The first famous clue was not recovered in a modern excavation. A local monk found a partial human jaw in nineteen eighty.
14,130,140,2,BS08_013;BS08_015,cut,That discovery eventually became known as the Xiahe mandible. Its original position within the cave's layered sediments was not recorded by archaeologists.
15,140,150,2,BS08_015;BS08_019,cut,"The missing position mattered. Without it, researchers could not simply point to an excavation layer and read the jaw's age from its surroundings."
16,150,160,2,BS08_016;@AGE_MIN,cut,"Instead, they studied a carbonate crust attached to the fossil. Uranium series measurements supplied a minimum age of about one hundred sixty thousand years."
17,160,170,2,@AGE_MIN;BS08_013,cut,Minimum is the crucial word. The result establishes that the jaw is at least that old; it does not give an exact death date.
18,170,180,2,BS08_017;BS08_025,cut,Then came another obstacle. Ancient genetic material does not always survive in a form that researchers can recover and confidently interpret today.
19,180,190,2,BS08_018;BS08_013,cut,"In two thousand nineteen, a study used surviving ancient proteins to connect this mandible with Denisovans, an ancient human lineage first identified through genetics."
20,190,200,2,@MAP;BS08_004,cut,"The result moved the story beyond one Siberian cave. Denisovan evidence now reached a high plateau, far from the place that gave them their name."
21,200,210,2,BS08_019;BS08_096,cut,"Later research recovered Denisovan mitochondrial DNA from cave sediments. Those microscopic traces added evidence from different periods, rather than a single isolated fossil."
22,210,220,2,BS08_013;BS08_020,cut,A lineage had become visible. A life had not. A jaw and molecules could establish presence without showing what anyone ate for dinner.
23,220,230,2,BS08_020;BS08_023,cut,"To approach that question, investigators needed a different collection: the fragmented animals left behind in the cave, and the marks preserved on them."
24,230,240,2,BS08_024;BS08_110,cut,"Most pieces looked far less dramatic than a human fossil. That was precisely why the next discovery required such patient, systematic attention."
25,240,250,3,BS08_022;@COUNTS,cut,"A study published in two thousand twenty four examined two thousand five hundred sixty seven faunal specimens, using anatomy alongside surviving molecular evidence."
26,250,260,3,BS08_023;BS08_024,cut,"These were specimens and fragments, not a count of individual animals. Several pieces can come from one body; one piece can resist identification."
27,260,270,3,BS08_024;BS08_057,cut,"A small shaft fragment may have lost the features that distinguish a leg bone. To the eye, many mammals can begin to look alike."
28,270,280,3,BS08_025;@COLLAGEN,cut,"Collagen, a structural protein in bone, can preserve a different kind of clue. Its surviving fragments can help narrow an unknown specimen's identity."
29,280,290,3,BS08_026;BS08_018,cut,"The screening method is called ZooMS. It compares patterns from collagen peptides, giving investigators a practical way to sort many otherwise uninformative fragments."
30,290,300,3,@COUNTS;BS08_022,cut,"In this study, one thousand eight hundred fifty seven specimens entered that screening process. Anatomy and molecular work together identified much of the collection."
31,300,310,3,@CAPRINES;BS08_028,cut,"The dominant group was caprines, especially bharal, often called blue sheep. Their importance was written repeatedly across an archive of broken animal bones."
32,310,320,3,BS08_028;BS08_007,cut,"Their coats are muted grey and brown, suited to rocky country. These animals can move across slopes that make a human approach demanding."
33,320,330,3,BS08_070;BS08_073,cut,"The collection also included other large herbivores, smaller mammals, birds, and carnivores. It offered a view of resources and environments around the cave."
34,330,340,3,BS08_030;@MOSAIC,cut,"A list of species is only the beginning. An animal's presence does not automatically mean that people hunted it, butchered it, or ate it."
35,340,350,3,BS08_040;BS08_045,cut,"To connect the animals with human choices, investigators needed the next layer of evidence: what had actually happened to the bones themselves."
36,350,360,4,BS08_031;BS08_029,dissolve,"Before looking at those marks, picture the problem outside. Food was spread through a landscape of slopes, open ground, and changing patches of vegetation."
37,360,370,4,BS08_032;BS08_033,cut,A moving animal is a moving opportunity. Reaching it requires time and effort; bringing useful material back creates another set of physical costs.
38,370,380,4,BS08_033;BS08_038,cut,We cannot reconstruct a particular hunt from this collection. We can recognise that terrain would shape which opportunities were practical to pursue.
39,380,390,4,BS08_032;BS08_031,cut,"Consider a mature bharal crossing a steep slope. Its movement makes clear why success would require attention to ground, distance, and animal behaviour."
40,390,400,4,BS08_035;BS08_057,cut,"Now consider a heavy limb bone. Its useful contents may travel differently from an entire carcass, changing what is worth carrying to shelter."
41,400,410,4,BS08_036;BS08_023,cut,"Archaeologists investigate such possibilities through which body parts survive, their condition, and their context. No single fragment supplies an entire transport plan."
42,410,420,4,BS08_037;BS08_084,cut,"Cold and exertion can make reliable energy especially valuable. Yet the record does not reveal a daily menu, a calorie total, or every season."
43,420,430,4,BS08_038;BS08_069,cut,That uncertainty does not erase the practical question. A resource can be abundant in a valley and still be expensive to obtain and process.
44,430,440,4,BS08_039;BS08_103,cut,"At high elevation, choices connect. Time spent searching affects energy spent walking; useful material brought back affects what can happen at the cave."
45,440,450,4,BS08_031;BS08_079,cut,These relationships turn the landscape into more than scenery. It becomes part of the problem that ancient people had to solve with their bodies.
46,450,460,4,BS08_040;BS08_043,cut,"The cave's animal fragments bring us closer to the solution, because some carry traces that a living animal could never have made itself."
47,460,470,5,BS08_040;BS08_115,dissolve,"Under magnification, a cut can become a narrow groove with fine internal features. Its location can help indicate which processing action produced it."
48,470,480,5,BS08_047;BS08_042,cut,A sharp stone edge drawn across tissue may contact bone beneath. Repeated movements can leave marks near places where muscles or joints were worked.
49,480,490,5,BS08_043;BS08_053,cut,A blow produces a different record. Notches and other percussion features can preserve evidence that someone struck a bone while extracting useful resources.
50,490,500,5,BS08_044;@MARKS,cut,"But bones can break without people. Carnivore teeth, trampling, weathering, and pressure after burial can also modify a specimen's surface or shape."
51,500,510,5,BS08_045;BS08_077,cut,That is why researchers compare combinations of features and their context. Calling every scratch a knife mark would create a story the evidence cannot support.
52,510,520,5,BS08_023;BS08_089,cut,"At Baishiya, the study documented hundreds of specimens with human modifications. The collection contained more than animals that simply happened to die nearby."
53,520,530,5,BS08_020;BS08_112,cut,"Marks supported activities involving meat, marrow, and hides. They connected ancient people with particular resources, even without preserving the full sequence of events."
54,530,540,5,BS08_047;BS08_054,cut,"Imagine an adult demonstrator working a stone edge across a dry teaching model. The movement illustrates a mechanism, not a recovered moment from prehistory."
55,540,550,5,BS08_040;BS08_043,cut,The real force of the evidence lies in repetition across specimens. A pattern of processing tells us more than one dramatic looking broken bone.
56,550,560,5,BS08_049;BS08_035,cut,"It also changes the question. Instead of asking only which animals were nearby, we can ask how people turned parts of those animals into resources."
57,560,570,5,BS08_050;BS08_059,cut,"And some of the most valuable material was hidden behind a hard outer wall, where a cutting edge alone would not be enough."
58,570,580,6,BS08_051;@MARROW,cut,"Inside many limb bones lies marrow, a tissue that can contain substantial fat. Accessing it requires opening something built to withstand considerable force."
59,580,590,6,BS08_051;BS08_056,cut,Fat supplies concentrated dietary energy. Its practical importance helps explain why a bone could remain useful after much of the visible meat was removed.
60,590,600,6,BS08_053;BS08_043,cut,"A fresh bone can respond to a controlled blow differently from a dry, weathered one. Fracture characteristics help researchers investigate how breakage occurred."
61,600,610,6,@MARKS;BS08_053,cut,"No individual mark is a perfect recording. Together with percussion damage and other evidence, however, fresh breakage can support an interpretation of deliberate processing."
62,610,620,6,BS08_054;BS08_047,cut,"The action is simple to describe, harder to perform well. Position the object, choose a striking point, and direct force toward the intended result."
63,620,630,6,BS08_055;BS08_054,cut,"For our reconstruction, an anonymous adult uses a clean animal bone replica. There is no blood, no invented kill, and no identified ancient individual."
64,630,640,6,BS08_056;BS08_059,cut,The demonstration shows why knowledge matters. A resource hidden inside hard material becomes available only when a body and a technique work together.
65,640,650,6,BS08_057;BS08_112,cut,"At Baishiya, marrow processing sits within a wider pattern of animal use. It is one part of the survival story, rather than the whole answer."
66,650,660,6,BS08_015;BS08_082,cut,"We should not turn that pattern into a claim that every meal was identical. The record samples events across time, with gaps and uneven preservation."
67,660,670,6,BS08_059;BS08_051,cut,"Still, the broken fragments reveal something powerful: usefulness did not end at the surface. People could obtain resources that were physically difficult to reach."
68,670,680,6,BS08_060;BS08_062,cut,"And food was not the only possible reward. Other traces point toward animal materials being handled, worked, and brought into another cycle of use."
69,680,690,7,BS08_062;BS08_112,dissolve,"A hide can become a useful material after careful removal and preparation. Cut marks can contribute evidence for that work, depending on their position."
70,690,700,7,BS08_062;@HIDES,cut,"Such evidence does not preserve an entire garment. We cannot supply exact clothing designs, sewing techniques, or personal styles that the cave has not revealed."
71,700,710,7,BS08_109;@HIDES,cut,The distinction matters because an attractive reconstruction can quietly become a false memory. A convincing coat on screen is still an illustrator's choice.
72,710,720,7,BS08_063;@TOOLS,cut,"The bones themselves provide a firmer next step. The study described three expedient bone tools, made from material already available during animal processing."
73,720,730,7,BS08_064;BS08_068,cut,"Expedient does not mean meaningless. A broken shaft can offer an edge or shape useful for a task without becoming an elaborate, decorated implement."
74,730,740,7,BS08_065;BS08_063,cut,"Researchers examined working traces and form to investigate these objects. They also reported a possible retoucher made from an equid tooth, with appropriate caution."
75,740,750,7,BS08_065;@TOOLS,cut,Possible remains possible. A tentative interpretation should not turn into a proven tool simply because a close camera shot makes it look persuasive.
76,750,760,7,BS08_066;BS08_112,cut,"Together, these finds widen the chain: an animal can supply food, hide, and material for further work, rather than a single disposable product."
77,760,770,7,BS08_067;BS08_119,cut,"This is a practical form of flexibility. Different parts can answer different needs, although the surviving fragments cannot reveal every choice or its purpose."
78,770,780,7,BS08_068;BS08_063,cut,Picture a small teaching replica rotating under neutral light. Its modest size makes the point: a useful innovation does not have to look spectacular.
79,780,790,7,BS08_069;BS08_029,cut,"At Baishiya, the next surprise comes from looking beyond the dominant prey group, into the less common animals scattered through the collection."
80,790,800,8,BS08_070;BS08_074,dissolve,"Wild yak, gazelle, and equids contributed to the faunal picture. Their presence fits with a landscape containing substantial areas of open, grassy ground."
81,800,810,8,BS08_069;BS08_113,cut,"Other remains suggested nearby woodland or shrub patches. Deer, small mammals, and other animals complicated any image of a single empty, frozen wilderness."
82,810,820,8,BS08_069;@MOSAIC,cut,"The surroundings were a mosaic. Different habitats could offer different opportunities, even though their arrangement and availability changed through long stretches of time."
83,820,830,8,BS08_073;BS08_023,cut,"Some bones from smaller mammals, birds, and carnivores also carried human modifications. The documented processing extended beyond a picture of large herbivores alone."
84,830,840,8,BS08_074;BS08_079,cut,"That breadth matters. Depending entirely on one opportunity can create vulnerability when conditions change, whereas using different resources can offer additional possibilities."
85,840,850,8,BS08_083;@MOSAIC,cut,"But the collection cannot prove every species was deliberately hunted. Some remains may have arrived through other animals, natural events, or different human activities."
86,850,860,8,BS08_076;BS08_024,cut,"Even an impressive animal bone needs its own context. Size alone cannot tell us who killed an animal, how it died, or why it arrived."
87,860,870,8,BS08_077;BS08_089,cut,"The method is to keep two questions separate: which animals are represented, and which specimens preserve evidence of people working with their remains."
88,870,880,8,BS08_040;BS08_083,cut,"When those questions meet, the story becomes stronger. Human behaviour emerges from the modified material, rather than from a dramatic animal added to a scene."
89,880,890,8,BS08_079;BS08_080,cut,"This approach also keeps the plateau alive as a changing place. Open slopes and sheltered patches could each matter, without making every ancient period identical."
90,890,900,8,BS08_080;BS08_015,cut,"We have evidence for flexibility in resource use. To understand its scale, we must now confront something a single survival scene tends to hide: time."
91,900,910,9,BS08_015;@LONG_TIME,cut,The cave's deposits accumulated across a very long interval. The studied sequence spans roughly one hundred ninety thousand to thirty thousand years ago.
92,910,920,9,BS08_082;@LONG_TIME,cut,"That is an archaeological framework, not proof that the same population occupied the entrance continuously. Layers can contain gaps, mixed traces, and unequal samples."
93,920,930,9,BS08_083;BS08_020,cut,The oldest sampled deposits supplied many animal fragments. Their makers cannot all be assigned a biological identity simply by borrowing evidence from younger layers.
94,930,940,9,BS08_019;@EVIDENCE,cut,Denisovan fossils and sediment molecules anchor parts of the sequence. Those anchors are stronger than a claim that every ancient visitor was already identified.
95,940,950,9,BS08_084;BS08_085,cut,Nor can repeated presence automatically establish residence through every winter. Seasonal visits and longer stays are different possibilities that require their own evidence.
96,950,960,9,BS08_085;@FIRE,cut,Then there is the obvious survival question: fire. It is tempting to place a bright hearth inside every reconstruction of a cold mountain cave.
97,960,970,9,@FIRE;BS08_045,cut,"Burned bones were found, but the available evidence was limited. The study could not resolve whether that burning came from human activity or natural causes."
98,970,980,9,BS08_085;BS08_082,cut,So we leave that particular hearth out of the reconstruction. A question can remain open without making the well supported processing evidence disappear.
99,980,990,9,BS08_088;BS08_092,cut,Good archaeology does not make every gap look complete. It builds a picture that can grow more detailed when another test or discovery arrives.
100,990,1000,9,BS08_089;BS08_091,cut,"At Baishiya, one such discovery was waiting inside the sorting process itself. It was small, broken, and initially surrounded by the remains of other animals."
101,1000,1010,9,BS08_091;BS08_097,cut,"The collection was about to connect the resource story with a human body again, this time through something less recognisable than a jaw."
102,1010,1020,10,BS08_091;BS08_017,dissolve,"A short rib fragment entered the molecular screening. The initial collagen evidence placed it among close human relatives, making it a candidate for further investigation."
103,1020,1030,10,BS08_092;BS08_018,cut,"That first screen did not, by itself, deliver the complete identification. Researchers followed it with a more detailed analysis of surviving ancient proteins."
104,1030,1040,10,@RIB;BS08_025,cut,"The result identified the fragment as Denisovan. The specimen, called Xiahe two, brought a new human fossil into the Baishiya evidence record."
105,1040,1050,10,BS08_015;@RIB,cut,"It came from a layer dated to roughly forty eight thousand to thirty two thousand years ago, far younger than the first jaw's minimum age."
106,1050,1060,10,@LIMIT;BS08_097,cut,"Those are the dates of its sedimentary context. They should not become an exact birthday, an exact death date, or a date for a lineage's extinction."
107,1060,1070,10,BS08_096;BS08_089,cut,The fragment had broken into two pieces during excavation. Even modern recovery can add a fresh event to an object's much longer history.
108,1070,1080,10,BS08_096;BS08_019,cut,"What made this find powerful was the combination: an excavated context, a molecular identification, and animal evidence from the surrounding cave sequence."
109,1080,1090,10,BS08_003;BS08_098,cut,A familiar label had returned through an unfamiliar object. The same broad human lineage was now represented in another part of the site's long record.
110,1090,1100,10,BS08_097;@LIMIT,cut,"We still cannot give this person a face, a voice, or a personal story. A rib can establish a connection without supplying a biography."
111,1100,1110,10,BS08_098;BS08_004,cut,Yet the connection matters. The mountain was more than a distant dot on a distribution map; it formed part of a repeated Denisovan presence.
112,1110,1120,10,BS08_037;BS08_103,cut,"That brings us to the most tempting question of all. Did their bodies carry a special solution to altitude, alongside the behaviours preserved in bone?"
113,1120,1130,11,@EPAS1;BS08_105,cut,Modern Tibetan populations offer an important genetic clue. Research connected a region around the gene called E P A S one with Denisovan related ancestry.
114,1130,1140,11,BS08_101;@EPAS1,cut,"This region is associated with aspects of adaptation to high altitude, including haemoglobin differences. It links ancient population history with biology in living people."
115,1140,1150,11,BS08_102;@BODY_KNOWLEDGE,cut,It does not tell us the complete physiology of the Xiahe individuals. The mandible and rib do not provide a full recovered genome for that purpose.
116,1150,1160,11,BS08_103;BS08_005,cut,"Nor should one gene become a universal superpower. Living at altitude involves bodies, development, environment, experience, and behaviour, not a single magical switch."
117,1160,1170,11,@EVIDENCE;BS08_105,cut,The genetic evidence and the archaeological evidence answer different questions. One concerns inherited variation in modern populations; the other concerns ancient presence and activities.
118,1170,1180,11,BS08_105;BS08_017,cut,Keeping them distinct lets each remain useful. We can discuss a Denisovan related contribution without inventing a laboratory measurement of an ancient person's breathing.
119,1180,1190,11,BS08_106;BS08_113,cut,"Return instead to the practical world outside the entrance. Whatever their physiology, people still needed resources that their bodies could obtain and use."
120,1190,1200,11,BS08_107;BS08_035,cut,"They still encountered terrain, changing habitats, and the demands of processing material. Genetic inheritance could not strike a bone or carry an object for them."
121,1200,1210,11,BS08_119;BS08_112,cut,"The evidence we can see concerns actions and connections: animals brought into the record, materials modified, useful parts accessed, and fragments made into tools."
122,1210,1220,11,BS08_109;@BODY_KNOWLEDGE,cut,"That combination offers a richer survival story than a heroic body alone. It places knowledge and decisions beside biological history, while leaving unmeasured details open."
123,1220,1230,11,BS08_110;BS08_024,cut,"And it prepares us to understand why the smallest fragments deserve attention. Their importance comes from the relationships they preserve, not from their size."
124,1230,1240,12,BS08_008;BS08_003,dissolve,"At the beginning, the cave seemed to pose a single question: how could ancient people manage life on a demanding high plateau at all?"
125,1240,1250,12,BS08_112;@RECAP,cut,"The answer has emerged in pieces. Animal resources, deliberate processing, hidden marrow, useful hides, and worked bone reveal parts of a practical response."
126,1250,1260,12,BS08_113;BS08_029,cut,"The collection also reveals flexibility across resources and habitats. It does not promise that every attempt succeeded, or that difficult conditions ever stopped being difficult."
127,1260,1270,12,BS08_114;BS08_015,cut,Survival leaves an uneven archive. Successful actions may leave fragments; failed journeys may leave nothing we can identify at the cave entrance today.
128,1270,1280,12,BS08_115;BS08_110,cut,That is why these remains should not become a complete adventure movie mistaken for history. Their real story is powerful enough without invented victories.
129,1280,1290,12,BS08_013;BS08_019;BS08_091;@EVIDENCE,cut,A partial jaw established an ancient connection. Sediment molecules added other traces. A rib among animal fragments brought another Denisovan individual into view.
130,1290,1300,12,BS08_117;BS08_040,cut,"Around those human clues, the modified animal remains supplied something identity alone could not: evidence for how resources were used in this place."
131,1300,1310,12,BS08_118;BS08_012,cut,The plateau stayed demanding. The cave stayed a shelter rather than a guarantee. What changed was our ability to recognise the choices preserved there.
132,1310,1320,12,BS08_040;BS08_009,cut,A cut across bone can outlast the hand that made it. A fracture can preserve an action long after the reason for that action disappears.
133,1320,1330,12,BS08_119;BS08_107,cut,"At Baishiya, those small traces open a vast human question: how knowledge, bodies, and changing environments met in the effort to continue living."
134,1330,1340,12,BS08_120,cut,"The people are gone from the entrance. Their broken fragments remain, turning a mountain cave into evidence that an ancient world was once someone's practical reality."
